# WI-16 T1.3 — provisioning the fixture, and the first green run

T1.3 does two outward-facing things under the owner's authority, given 2026-09-25:
creates `manjula25/loop-integration-fixture` (public) and files its seed issue.

## 1. The seed, built locally before anything was created

Seven files under `/tmp/wi16-seed/loop-integration-fixture`, committed as one seed
commit. `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` was run **before** `git init` and failed
("not a git repository"), per the repository's lesson about un-initialized
directories silently joining a parent repo.

```
$ git log --format='%H%n%an <%ae>%n%s' -1
a4348dafa4aa328b908692ed46a1d4ddf9796fd7
manjula <manjula@bitcot.com>
seed: loopsample with a latent truncate defect

$ git ls-files
.gitignore
.loop-harness/profile.json
README.md
pyproject.toml
src/loopsample/__init__.py
src/loopsample/textops.py
tests/test_textops.py
```

**Born-green and latent-defect, checked before the repository existed** — run in
`sandcastle-loop` against a read-only mount of the seed:

```
$ docker run --rm --user 0:0 -v <seed>:/src:ro --entrypoint bash sandcastle-loop -lc \
    'cp -r /src /work && cd /work && pip install -q -e ".[test]" && pytest -q; ...'
...                                                                      [100%]
3 passed in 0.01s
PYTEST_EXIT=0
defect probe: 'abcde…' len=6
docstring example: 'abcd…'
at-limit: 'abcde'
```

The contract suite is green **with the defect present** (D3), and the defect is
real: `truncate("abcdefghij", 5)` returns six characters. The docstring states the
correct contract (`truncate("abcdef", 4) == "abc…"`) and the code violates it, so
the issue body's stated symptom and its expected value are both accurate.

## 2. The repository, read back rather than assumed

```
$ gh repo view manjula25/loop-integration-fixture --json name,visibility,defaultBranchRef,isEmpty
{"defaultBranchRef":{"name":"main"},"isEmpty":false,"name":"loop-integration-fixture","visibility":"PUBLIC"}

$ git ls-remote --symref origin HEAD
ref: refs/heads/main	HEAD
a4348dafa4aa328b908692ed46a1d4ddf9796fd7	HEAD
```

`isEmpty: false`, and the remote's `HEAD` is the local seed commit — the push
landed what was committed, not something else.

## 3. The issue, read back

```
$ gh issue list --repo manjula25/loop-integration-fixture --state open --json number,title,labels
[{"labels":[],"number":1,"title":"truncate() returns one character more than the limit"}]
```

Exactly one open issue, **#1** — no labels, no attachment URL, and no cause stated.
`#1` normalizes to id `gh-1`, so scenario 1's reproduction test is
`tests/fixed-issues/test_gh_1.py`.

## 4. The first run: RED, and not on its subject

The first `npm run test:integration` after provisioning **failed** — and the
failure was in T1.2's own test, not in the fixture:

```
$ npm run test:integration
Cloning into '/tmp/loop-integration-fixture'...
 ❯ tests/integration/fixture.test.ts (3 tests | 1 failed) 16844ms
   ❯ the disposable integration fixture (WI-16 T1) (3)
     × has a contract suite that is green in the production image 13839ms

Error: Test timed out in 5000ms.
 Test Files  1 failed (1)
      Tests  1 failed | 2 passed (3)
exit=1
```

A cold `gh repo clone` plus a container run with `pip install` needs ~14s; vitest's
default is 5000ms. The test could never have passed. Fixed by raising
`testTimeout` to 300_000 in `vitest.integration.config.ts` — see that file's comment
for why the value is a bound rather than a budget.

That run's output above is transcribed from the terminal capture; its raw file was
overwritten by the green run in §5, so it is not re-readable from disk.

This RED is recorded rather than discarded because it is the honest history of the
first run against the real fixture. It is not the guard's RED (that is
`t1-guard-absent-fixture.log`) and not FR-004's killed-run criterion (that needs a
run that does work, and completes in T4).

## 5. The green run

```
$ npm run test:integration
 Test Files  1 passed (1)
      Tests  3 passed (3)
   Start at  20:53:12
   Duration  8.74s (tests 99%)
exit=0
```

All three T1 tests pass against the live fixture.

## 6. Unchanged, checked in the same step

```
$ npm test          → Test Files 10 passed (10); Tests 287 passed (287); exit=0
$ npm run typecheck → exit 0
```

The integration surface is additive: the default gate is byte-for-byte the same
suite it was before T1.1.